  <Id>b7f6d3a2-4c1e-4e8a-9f2b-7d5c0a1e6f43</Id>
  <!--
    THIS IS NOT THE npm VERSION AND CANNOT BE. Office rejects a manifest
    version below 1.0 outright ("unsupported version number less than 1.0"),
    and package.json is on 0.x — so the two schemes are independent and this
    one is maintained by hand.

    BUMP IT ON EVERY RELEASE THAT CHANGES THIS FILE. It sat at 1.0.0.0 across
    v0.4.0, v0.5.0 and v0.6.0 while the file itself changed, which is a real
    hole rather than an untidiness: a centrally-deployed or sideloaded add-in
    is refreshed on the strength of this number, so a manifest that changes
    without it can go on serving the cached one. 1.0.1.0 is the first bump, for
    v0.6.1 — the release whose entire content is a change to this file's
    requirement set, and therefore exactly the release that must reach people.
  -->
  <Version>1.0.1.0</Version>
  <ProviderName>SSF Charts (open source)</ProviderName>
  <DefaultLocale>en-US</DefaultLocale>
  <DisplayName DefaultValue="SSF Charts" />
  <!--
    THIS STRING IS STORE-FACING, and it promised "inserted as native, fully
    editable PowerPoint shapes" with no qualifier until 2026-09-20.

    On PowerPoint on the WEB that is not true of every chart, and the web is
    where a certifier tests. A chart above DEMO_SHAPE_BUDGET (105 native shapes)
    goes onto the slide as a picture instead — `wantsAutoPicture`, gated on
    `isWebHost()`, so it happens on the web and nowhere else. Measured over the
    25 samples the picker offers, two cross it: Tile map (122) and Area (111).
    "Functionality does not match the offer description" is the ordinary
    AppSource rejection, and this field IS part of the offer description.

    The qualifier is about the HOST AND THE DENSITY, not about the build's age.
    An earlier attempt at this in `docs/STORE-LISTING.md` qualified by build age
    ("older builds get..."), which the 1.10 floor makes impossible: a build that
    cannot draw the chart cannot install the add-in.

    221 characters, under Microsoft's documented 250 for Description.
    `docs/STORE-LISTING.md` carries the measurement and the matching listing
    copy; `test/web-density-census.test.ts` re-derives the numbers so they
    cannot go stale quietly.
  -->
  <Description DefaultValue="Consulting-grade charts — waterfall/bridge, Mekko/Marimekko, stacked columns, CAGR and difference arrows — drawn as native, fully editable PowerPoint shapes; the densest layouts arrive as a re-editable picture on the web." />
  <IconUrl DefaultValue="https://ssf-chart.struktureretsundfornuft.dk/assets/icon-32.png" />
  <HighResolutionIconUrl DefaultValue="https://ssf-chart.struktureretsundfornuft.dk/assets/icon-64.png" />
  <SupportUrl DefaultValue="https://github.com/dannbleeker/SSF-Charts" />

  <Hosts>
    <Host Name="Presentation" />
  </Hosts>

  <Requirements>
    <Sets DefaultMinVersion="1.1">
      <!--
        1.4 DRAWS A CHART; 1.8 IS WHERE ONE ARRIVES AS A CHART; 1.10 IS WHERE
        EVERY CHART ARRIVES WHOLE. Raised 1.4 -> 1.8 and then 1.8 -> 1.10 on
        2026-09-13, both deliberately, and this is the reasoning so nobody
        lowers it back on reach grounds alone.

        1.4 is what the shapes need (ShapeCollection.addGeometricShape / addLine
        / addTextBox) and it is not enough. `Shape.group` needs 1.8, so below it
        a chart lands as ~40 LOOSE SHAPES with nothing said about it. 1.10 adds
        `Shape.rotation`, without which `addWedgeFan` and the arrowhead case
        trace and return NOTHING: measured over the 123 shipped showcase charts,
        18 lose ink below 1.10 and 9 lose their SUBJECT — every pie, every
        doughnut, every sunburst and the radial-bar radar keep only their
        labels. (`test/below-1-10-census.test.ts` re-derives those counts; it is
        how the published figure was caught at 8 when it is 9.)

        WHY THE FLOOR IS THE ONLY LEVER, which is the part that is easy to get
        backwards. There is NO way to scope an add-in by platform:
        `<Host Name="Presentation" />` means web + Windows + Mac + iPad as one
        unit, and Microsoft says so outright — "You can't explicitly specify the
        Office versions and builds or the platforms on which your add-in should
        be installable". office-js#6658 is an open report from a publisher stuck
        on exactly this, and it names the mechanism: Partner Center AUTO-DERIVES
        the certified platform list from this Requirements block. Certification
        policy 1120.3 then reads "Add-ins must work across all platforms that
        support methods defined in the Requirements element... Add-ins must
        support Office on web and Mac applications compatible with the APIs
        specified in the manifest."

        So a LOWER floor does not hedge. It ENLARGES the surface being
        certified.

        WHAT 1.10 COSTS, measured against Microsoft's update history on
        2026-09-13: nothing on any serviced Microsoft 365 channel. 1.10 needs
        Windows 2601 (19610.20002); Current is 2608 (20326.20144) and both
        Monthly Enterprise and Semi-Annual Enterprise are on 2608/2607/2606.
        Retail perpetual Office 2024 is on the same 2608 train. On Mac 1.10
        needs 16.105 (13 Jan 2026) and the current release is 16.112.4 (10 Sep
        2026) — and that page states "Only the most recently released version
        listed below is supported", so the 16.96-16.104 band this excludes is
        not a supported population at all.

        WHAT 1.8 WAS COSTING, which is what moved the number. Below 1.10
        `canRotate()` is false, and at a 1.8 floor the ONLY hosts that could
        install and answer false were Mac 16.96-16.104 — the web reports 1.10,
        every serviced Windows channel is past 2601, and volume-licensed/LTSC
        (capped at 1.5) and iPad (capped at 1.1) cannot install at either floor.
        That made the entire picture-fallback path Mac-only code on the one
        platform this project has never measured. At 1.10 it is unreachable on
        every host the manifest admits, so the certified platforms all run the
        same path — the one measured across 425+ web rounds and one Windows
        desktop reading.

        An earlier version of this comment defended 1.8 on the grounds that
        "the extra exclusion 1.10 would add is Mac 16.96-16.104, which is a live,
        updating population". That was wrong on its own terms: a Mac that is
        updating has long since passed 16.105, and Microsoft supports only the
        newest. Kept here because the argument is the thing worth preserving.
      -->
      <Set Name="PowerPointApi" MinVersion="1.10" />
    </Sets>
  </Requirements>

